@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction

  Tessera X collects mail, news, and feed content into context
snapshots.  This chapter introduces the result, the required
applications, and a first preparation command.

@menu
* Overview::            Context snapshots and their sources.
* Requirements::        Installing and loading the package.
* First Snapshot::      Preparing and viewing a first result.
@end menu

@node Overview
@section Overview
@cindex experimental features
@cindex context snapshots
  Tessera X provides experimental features for Gnus, mu4e, and Elfeed.
The @samp{x} means @dfn{experimental}; the feature implemented in this
version is content aggregation through context snapshots.

  A @dfn{context snapshot} collects selected records, their metadata,
and available body text in a separate, read-only buffer.  You can
inspect it, copy its text, or use it in another application.  Content
comes from native records and available source text, independently of
the abbreviated text displayed in message lists.  Preparing a snapshot
does not send a language-model request.

  The @dfn{source buffer} is the buffer from which you start
preparation.  The @dfn{scope} is the set of messages or entries selected
for that snapshot: for example, marked messages, replies to the current
article, or today's entries in one feed.  Each snapshot belongs to the
request that created it; later changes to marks or search filters do not
change its contents.

  For commands and options, see @ref{Context Snapshots}.  For Lisp
interfaces that consume snapshots or provide another source of content,
see @ref{Programming Contexts}.

@node Requirements
@section Requirements
@cindex installation
  Tessera X requires Emacs 30.1 or later.  It is independent of the
Tessera display package and Alert.  Install and configure the
application whose content you want to collect:

@table @asis
@item Gnus
Gnus is included with Emacs.  Enable Gnus Agent and configure it for the
groups you intend to use.  All Gnus snapshot commands require the Agent;
see @ref{Gnus Download Policy} for body retrieval and @ref{Gnus
Unplugged,,, gnus, The Gnus Manual} for Agent setup.

@item mu4e
Use mu4e 1.14.3 or later, with its mail paths, mu executable, and local
index configured.  Make its libraries available to Emacs.  Snapshots
read local mail; see @ref{mu4e Local Retrieval}.

@item Elfeed
Use Elfeed 4.0.1 or later, with its libraries and feed database
available to Emacs.  Today scopes also support Tree buffers provided by
an extension using @code{elfeed-tree-mode}; Tessera X does not provide
that interface.  See @ref{Today Scopes}.
@end table

  The libraries @code{tessera-x-gnus}, @code{tessera-x-mu4e}, and
@code{tessera-x-elfeed} are all included in Tessera X.  Loading one
exposes its options without loading the native application; commands
load their required application libraries when invoked.  No Tessera X
minor mode or default key bindings are installed.

  For a source checkout, add @file{lisp/tessera-x/} to @code{load-path}.
For example, replace the checkout path before evaluating:

@lisp
(add-to-list 'load-path "/path/to/emacs-tessera/lisp/tessera-x/")
(require 'tessera-x-gnus)
@end lisp

@noindent
Use the corresponding library for mu4e or Elfeed.  Use @kbd{M-x
customize-group @key{RET} tessera-x @key{RET}} for shared options, or
@code{tessera-x-gnus}, @code{tessera-x-mu4e}, and
@code{tessera-x-elfeed} for application-specific options.  Load the
owning library before setting its options in an initialization file.

@node First Snapshot
@section Preparing a First Snapshot
@cindex snapshot, preparing
  In a supported source buffer, move point to a message or entry and run
the corresponding command.  With no active region or native selection
marks, it prepares that record:

@table @asis
@item Gnus Summary
@kbd{M-x tessera-x-gnus-prepare-context}.

@item mu4e Headers
@kbd{M-x tessera-x-mu4e-prepare-context}.

@item Elfeed Search
@kbd{M-x tessera-x-elfeed-prepare-context}.
@end table

  By default, Gnus may download a missing body into its Agent, and
Elfeed may fetch a linked page.  mu4e reads local message files.  To
keep Gnus and Elfeed preparation local, configure the applicable library
before running the command:

@lisp
(require 'tessera-x-gnus)
(setopt tessera-x-gnus-body-policy 'local-only)

(require 'tessera-x-elfeed)
(setopt tessera-x-elfeed-fetch-linked-content nil)
@end lisp

@noindent
These settings control retrieval; they cannot supply content that is
absent locally.  See @ref{Retrieving Content} for the policies and their
scope.

  Once preparation has completed, run @kbd{M-x tessera-x-show-context}
in the source buffer to view the result.  Preparation itself does not
select the snapshot buffer.  Some requests finish before their command
returns; others continue in the background.  While a replacement is
pending, the show command still displays the previous successful
snapshot.  See @ref{Snapshot Lifecycle}.

@node Context Snapshots
@chapter Context Snapshots

  Preparing a context snapshot selects records, obtains available
content, and collects their metadata and body text in a result buffer.
The following sections explain each part of that task and the commands
for managing its results.

@menu
* Selecting Content::   Choosing records and their scope.
* Retrieving Content::  Reading local bodies or fetching content.
* Snapshot Contents::   Metadata, grouping, text, and size limits.
* Managing Snapshots: Snapshot Lifecycle.  Completion, viewing, and cancellation.
* Troubleshooting::     Diagnosing incomplete or unavailable results.
@end menu

@node Selecting Content
@section Selecting Content
@cindex selection
  Choose a command according to the scope you need: the current native
selection, a message and its replies, or today's locally indexed
records.  Selection uses native data, including folded mail and news
rows.  Display truncation does not limit the collected fields.

@menu
* Current Selection: Selected Messages and Entries.  Marks, the region, or the record at point.
* Subthreads::          A message and its descendants.
* Today Scopes::        Today's records in the native scope.
@end menu

@node Selected Messages and Entries
@subsection Selected Messages and Entries
@cindex marks
@cindex region
  The selection commands honor the source application's selection
rules.  Gnus gives the active region precedence over process marks;
mu4e and Elfeed give native marks precedence over the region.  Each
command returns a @code{tessera-x-context} request.  Its completion
depends on retrieval; see @ref{Request States}.

@deffn Command tessera-x-gnus-prepare-context
Prepare work articles in the current Gnus Summary buffer.  Use articles
in the active region, if any; otherwise use articles with process marks,
or the article at point when there are no such marks.  Signal a user
error outside Summary or when no articles are selected.
@end deffn

@deffn Command tessera-x-mu4e-prepare-context
Prepare marked messages in the current mu4e Headers buffer; if there are
no marks, use rows intersecting the active region; otherwise use the
message at point.  Include each selected message only once.  Signal a
user error outside Headers or when no message is selected.
@end deffn

@deffn Command tessera-x-elfeed-prepare-context
Prepare selected entries in the current Elfeed Search buffer.  Native
marks take precedence over the active region; otherwise select the entry
at point.  Signal a user error outside Search or for an empty selection.
@end deffn

  Selection preserves marks, tags, and read state.  It does not open
messages or execute pending mu4e operations.  Retrieving a Gnus body can
update its native availability mark; see @ref{Gnus Download Policy}.

@node Subthreads
@subsection Subthreads
@cindex descendants
@cindex subthreads
@cindex local index expansion
  A subthread consists of the current mail or news message and its
@dfn{descendants}: replies to that message, including replies to those
replies.  These commands retain the current message rather than
selecting all ancestors and siblings in its conversation.  Elfeed has no
subthread command.

  The ordinary scope is the current results, including folded rows.
Tessera follows reply references and, when @code{gnus-show-threads} or
@code{mu4e-search-threads} is non-@code{nil}, the application's native
thread structure.  With native threading disabled, it uses references
alone.  Equal subjects do not by themselves establish a relationship.

@deffn Command tessera-x-gnus-prepare-subthread-context &optional @
  local-index
Prepare the current Gnus Summary article and its descendants, returning
a context request.  A non-@code{nil} @var{local-index}, or an
interactive prefix such as @kbd{C-u}, supplements loaded records from
the current group's Agent overview.  Signal a user error outside
Summary or when there is no current article.
@end deffn

@defopt tessera-x-gnus-subthread-scope
The default Gnus subthread scope, initially @code{results}.  The
alternative @code{local-index} consults the current group's Agent
overview in addition to loaded results.
@end defopt

@cindex Agent overview
  The Agent overview is its local list of article headers.  Expansion
can find descendants outside a Summary filter, retaining selected
articles already in Summary without adding duplicates.  Missing or
stale overview data makes the selection incomplete.  Expansion does not
search a remote server for unknown descendants; subsequent body
retrieval follows @ref{Gnus Download Policy}.

@deffn Command tessera-x-mu4e-prepare-subthread-context &optional @
  local-index
Prepare the current mu4e Headers message and its descendants, returning
a context request.  A non-@code{nil} @var{local-index}, or an
interactive prefix such as @kbd{C-u}, supplements loaded records from
the local mu index.  Signal a user error outside Headers or when there
is no current message.
@end deffn

@defopt tessera-x-mu4e-subthread-scope
The default mu4e subthread scope, initially @code{results}.  The
alternative @code{local-index} supplements replies outside the current
result filter from the local index.
@end defopt

  mu4e expansion uses the current message's @samp{Message-ID} to find
related records.  It retains selected descendants already in the
results, adds descendants from the index, and removes duplicates.  A
missing identifier fails the request.  The Headers query remains
unchanged, and no mail is fetched from a server.  Unindexed mail and
unavailable relatives can still be absent.

  For either command, a non-@code{nil} argument requests expansion
regardless of the option.  A prefix does not switch back to
@code{results} when the option already selects @code{local-index}.
Both options can have global or buffer-local values and affect later
requests.  For synchronous and asynchronous completion, see
@ref{Request States}.

@node Today Scopes
@subsection Today Scopes
@cindex today scopes
@cindex offline retrieval
  Today commands select locally indexed records within the native scope
of the source buffer.  They never download Gnus bodies or overview data,
synchronize mail, update feeds, or fetch linked pages.  A valid scope
with no matches produces a ready empty snapshot; an unsupported buffer
or an invalid scope is an error.

@deffn Command tessera-x-gnus-prepare-today-context
Prepare today's local Agent articles, returning a context request.  In
Summary, use the current group's Agent overview.  In a Group buffer,
use the group or topic at point.  Signal a user error in unsupported
buffers or on a Group line identifying neither a group nor a topic.
@end deffn

@cindex topics
  A topic selects its own groups and its descendant topics' groups,
reading duplicate groups only once.  Selection consults their Agent
overviews rather than only loaded Summary rows.  Groups without an
agentized method or a readable overview contribute no records.  Records
without usable dates are excluded; the remaining records are sorted by
date before conversation grouping.

@deffn Command tessera-x-mu4e-prepare-today-context
Obtain a base query from @code{tessera-x-mu4e-today-query-function},
combine it with @samp{date:today..now}, and start an asynchronous local
index request.  Return the context request.
@end deffn

  The default query function reads the current Headers query or the
bookmark or maildir query item on the current Main line.  A Headers
buffer without a query, or a Main line without a query item, is a user
error.  This selects from the index, independently of the number of
currently displayed rows.

  A nonempty base produces @samp{(@var{base}) AND (date:today..now)};
an empty base uses @samp{date:today..now} alone.  mu interprets the date
range as today through now.  Tessera does not substitute a fixed
24-hour interval.

@defopt tessera-x-mu4e-today-query-function
A no-argument function returning the base mu query string.  The default
function reads the native Headers or Main scope described above.  It
runs in the source buffer before the request is created.
@end defopt

@cindex custom query functions
  A custom function can supply an account-specific scope or support
another source buffer.  It must return a string; an empty string leaves
today's query without an additional restriction.  For example, replace
the maildir below with one present in the configured index:

@lisp
(require 'tessera-x-mu4e)

(defun my-tessera-today-query ()
  "Return the mail scope for today's context."
  "maildir:/personal/Inbox")

(setopt tessera-x-mu4e-today-query-function
        #'my-tessera-today-query)
@end lisp

@noindent
This option can have a global or buffer-local value.  Changing it does
not alter a query already in progress.

@deffn Command tessera-x-elfeed-prepare-today-context
Prepare today's local entries and return a context request.  In Search,
use @code{elfeed-search-filter}.  In Tree, combine the Tree filter with
the native Elfeed filter associated with the current line.  Signal a
user error in unsupported buffers or on a Tree line without a filter.
@end deffn

  Elfeed visits its local database, including entries outside the
currently displayed rows.  Its ordinary filter parser and matcher
preserve restrictions such as tags and result limits.  The filter is
not rewritten.  A Tree line's displayed label alone does not specify a
filter.

@cindex local day boundaries
  Gnus and Elfeed include local midnight and exclude the following
midnight.  These calendar boundaries are computed when the command
runs.  Elfeed's native filter restrictions still apply within that
interval.  Gaps in local data remain gaps in the snapshot; see
@ref{Retrieving Content} for missing-body behavior.

@node Retrieving Content
@section Retrieving Content

  After selecting records, preparation reads the bodies available from
their application.  Gnus can fetch missing Agent bodies, mu4e reads
local mail files, and Elfeed can replace stored feed text with a linked
page.  Retrieval policies apply before the character budgets described
in @ref{Character Budgets}.

@menu
* Gnus Agent Bodies: Gnus Download Policy.  Local Agent bodies and optional downloads.
* Local Mail Files: mu4e Local Retrieval.  Mail files and local index queries.
* Linked Feed Pages: Elfeed Linked Pages.  Stored feed bodies and linked pages.
@end menu

@node Gnus Download Policy
@subsection Gnus Agent Bodies
@cindex Gnus Agent
@cindex body policy
  All Gnus snapshot commands read article bodies from the Agent.  The
Agent must be enabled, and local retrieval requires Agent data for the
group.  Downloading a missing body additionally requires an agentized
method.  Loading Tessera X does not configure those facilities.

@defopt tessera-x-gnus-body-policy
How selected-article and subthread requests obtain bodies.  The initial
value @code{download} uses native synchronous Agent fetching for absent
or empty bodies.  The value @code{local-only} reads existing Agent
bodies.  Today requests always use local-only retrieval, regardless of
this option.
@end defopt

  Downloads are grouped by newsgroup.  When they make bodies available
in the current Summary group, native availability marks are updated.
Read and process marks are retained, and the articles are not displayed.
Emacs waits for Agent downloads to finish before the command returns.

  This option can have a global or buffer-local value.  Changes affect
subsequent requests, not pending requests or existing snapshot buffers.
The local-only configuration in @ref{First Snapshot} applies to both
selected-article and subthread preparation.

@cindex missing bodies
  An inaccessible body receives the note @samp{Body absent from local
Agent}, unless a more specific download failure is already recorded.
A group's download failure is recorded on its affected items so that
other bodies remain usable.  Available metadata is retained even when
no body can be read.

@node mu4e Local Retrieval
@subsection Local Mail Files
@cindex mu find
@cindex missing message files
  mu4e snapshots resolve each selected message's readable local path
through mu4e and read the complete raw message.  They do not display the
message or alter its flags.  A missing or unreadable file becomes a
content note; metadata remains available even when the index refers to
a file that has moved or disappeared.

  Expanded subthreads and today scopes also query the local mu index.
Queries use @code{mu4e-mu-binary}.  A non-@code{nil} @code{mu4e-mu-home}
is expanded and passed as @samp{--muhome}; otherwise the executable uses
its default.  Query results are sorted by date with duplicates removed;
subthread expansion includes related messages.

  Index and body access can fail independently.  A query with no matches
produces an empty snapshot.  A failed query or unreadable query output
fails the request and reports the error.  An individual body-read
failure retains that record and its note.  The Headers query and result
buffer remain unchanged.

@node Elfeed Linked Pages
@subsection Linked Feed Pages
@cindex fetch eligibility
  Elfeed preparation first captures each entry's stored feed body.
Selected-entry requests can then fetch the entry's link and use the page
text in the snapshot.  This does not modify Elfeed's stored entry.
Today requests always use stored bodies and never fetch linked pages.

@defopt tessera-x-elfeed-fetch-linked-content
Whether selected-entry requests fetch linked pages, initially @code{t}.
A @code{nil} value uses stored feed content only.
@end defopt

@defopt tessera-x-elfeed-fetch-minimum-characters
A stored-body length at or above which retrieval is skipped, or
@code{nil} to fetch every eligible selected link.  Initially @code{nil}.
A nonnegative integer counts characters after any HTML feed content has
been rendered to text.
@end defopt

  Only links beginning with @samp{http://} or @samp{https://} enter the
queue.  With a numeric threshold, a shorter body is eligible and
equality skips fetching.  Zero skips all linked-page retrieval.  Entries
without an eligible link retain their stored content.

@cindex content types
  Successful responses must have a status in the 200--299 range and a
supported media type: @samp{text/html}, @samp{application/xhtml+xml}, or
@samp{text/plain}.  An absent Content-Type is treated as HTML.  The
declared charset is used when available, with UTF-8 as the fallback for
undecoded response bytes.

  HTML is rendered with SHR; plain text is trimmed at its boundaries.
A nonempty result replaces the item's body and receives the note
@samp{Fetched linked page}.  Metadata and provenance remain.  This is
not a specialized article extractor, so page navigation can remain in
the text.  See @ref{Text Extraction}.

@cindex HTTP fallback
  A startup error, unsupported media type, HTTP failure, empty result,
or timeout retains stored feed content.  The note begins @samp{Using
stored feed content:} and records the reason.  An individual failed page
does not fail the whole snapshot.  If no stored body exists, the entry's
metadata is still retained.  Attachment and enclosure URLs remain
metadata; their files are not downloaded as body text.

@cindex timeouts and concurrency
@defopt tessera-x-elfeed-fetch-timeout
Seconds allowed for an active linked-page transfer, initially @code{15}.
The value is a nonnegative integer.  Time in the queue is excluded;
expiration falls back to stored feed content.
@end defopt

@defopt tessera-x-elfeed-fetch-concurrency
Maximum active linked-page transfers for one request, initially
@code{4}.  The value is a nonnegative integer; values below one are used
as one.  Different source buffers have independent request limits.
@end defopt

  Eligibility, timeout, and concurrency are captured when a request is
built.  Options can have global or buffer-local values; changing them
does not reconfigure active requests.  Completing transfers in another
order does not reorder the snapshot.  Character budgets limit the
rendered result, not the HTTP transfers.

@node Snapshot Contents
@section Snapshot Contents

  The result contains source information and extracted body text, with
notes explaining unavailable or omitted content.  Grouping and character
budgets are applied when the snapshot is rendered.

@menu
* Metadata: Metadata and Grouping.  Source fields, grouping, and missing content.
* Text Extraction::     Converting mail and HTML bodies to text.
* Character Budgets::   Limiting total and per-body text.
@end menu

@node Metadata and Grouping
@subsection Metadata and Grouping
@cindex metadata
@cindex provenance
  A snapshot begins with the application name, creation time, scope,
and item count.  Each numbered item has a full subject or title and a
date, followed by available metadata and body text.  Unknown dates are
identified as such.  Ordinary metadata fields occupy separate lines;
body text retains its line breaks.

@table @asis
@item Mail and news
Items include Message-ID, References, any native inferred parent, full
addresses, and available labels.  Gnus retains newsgroup provenance and
combines registry marks, Gmail labels, and Keywords.  mu4e retains the
maildir and flags, and combines labels and tags without duplicates.
Reading raw mail can refine address and label header metadata.

@item Feeds
Items include entry and feed URLs, authors, tags, and enclosure URLs
and media types where available.  The printed group label is the feed
title.  These fields come from stored entry records.
@end table

@cindex conversation grouping
@cindex feed grouping
  Mail and news items are grouped by connected message identifiers,
reply references, and native parent relationships.  Equal subjects do
not merge unrelated conversations.  The first member's subject supplies
the group label.  Elfeed groups by feed URL rather than title.

  Groups occur in order of their first member, and each group retains
its input order.  An index expansion or date query can have a different
input order from the displayed results.  Grouping does not imply that
all members of a remote conversation have been discovered.

@cindex content notes
  A @samp{Content note} records unavailable bodies, extraction errors,
and retrieval fallbacks.  An item with no body can still provide useful
metadata.  Read these notes before treating a snapshot as complete.
An empty successful scope also produces a valid snapshot; commands
requiring a current selection instead reject an empty selection.

@node Text Extraction
@subsection Text Extraction
@cindex MIME
@cindex attachments
  Body extraction converts available message, feed, or page content to
plain text.  It retains quotations and signatures, and collects
attachment descriptions separately from body text.

  For a @samp{multipart/alternative} message, extraction prefers a
direct @samp{text/plain} alternative, then @samp{text/html}, then the
first available part.  Other multipart messages are traversed in MIME
order.  Plain text is decoded using its declared charset when possible;
HTML is rendered with SHR.

  Attachments contribute names and media types to metadata; their file
contents are not inserted as body text.  Other non-text parts are listed
by type.  Extraction disables signature verification and decryption.
An encrypted part contributes a notice that it has not been decrypted.
A MIME error or a message with no extractable body retains metadata and
an explanatory content note.

@cindex HTML conversion
  HTML conversion uses SHR at a width of 80 columns, with images and
font styling disabled.  It fetches neither images nor styles.  The
result is plain text with surrounding whitespace removed.  Rendering
can retain navigation and other surrounding text from a page.  Whether
Elfeed first fetches that page is controlled by @ref{Elfeed Linked
Pages}; conversion itself only processes the supplied content.

@node Character Budgets
@subsection Character Budgets
@cindex character budgets
  Budgets limit the body text included in a snapshot.  They count Emacs
characters, not bytes or language-model tokens.  They do not bound
network response size, index results, or the memory needed to read
source bodies before rendering.

@cindex metadata reservation
@defopt tessera-x-context-max-characters
The total character budget, initially @code{900000}.  Use a nonnegative
integer, or @code{nil} for no total limit.  The request captures its
value when it starts.
@end defopt

  Rendering reserves the introduction, full item metadata, boundaries,
and space for truncation notices before allocating body text.  The
remaining budget is divided among the remaining items in order.  Space
unused by a short body remains available to later items; this is not an
iterative redistribution among all bodies.

  Metadata and notices are never silently cut.  If they alone exceed the
budget, the snapshot can exceed the specified limit.  Reservations can
also leave some of the budget unused.

@cindex body truncation
@defopt tessera-x-context-body-max-characters
An additional maximum for each body, initially @code{nil}.  Use a
nonnegative integer, or @code{nil} for no separate per-body limit.  The
request captures this value when it starts.
@end defopt

  The actual allowance is the smallest of the body length, this limit
when set, and the body's share of the remaining total budget.  A cut
body is followed by @samp{[Body truncated by context budget.]}.  Setting
either budget to zero can suppress bodies while retaining metadata and
notices.

  Both options belong to the @code{tessera-x} customization group.  A
buffer-local binding affects requests started in that source buffer.
Changing an option does not modify pending requests or published
snapshots.

@node Snapshot Lifecycle
@section Managing Snapshots

  Each source buffer owns its current request and remembers its latest
successful snapshot.  Completion, viewing, and cancellation follow the
same rules for all applications.

@menu
* Completion: Request States.  Completion and pending requests.
* Viewing Snapshots: Managing Snapshots.  Viewing and discarding snapshots.
* Cancellation: Cancelling a Request.  Stopping work in the source buffer.
@end menu

@node Request States
@subsection Completion and Pending Requests
@cindex request states
@cindex asynchronous requests
  A request is @code{pending} while preparation remains in progress.
It becomes @code{ready} on success, @code{failed} if preparation cannot
complete, or @code{cancelled} if stopped.  Only a ready result replaces
the source buffer's latest snapshot.  Failure and cancellation leave
its previous successful result available.

  Commands differ in when they complete:

@table @asis
@item Gnus
All preparation is synchronous, including any Agent downloads.  The
command returns after publication or failure.

@item mu4e
Selected messages and subthreads limited to current results read their
files synchronously.  Index expansion and today scopes can remain
pending while a separate mu process runs.

@item Elfeed
Preparation with stored content only is synchronous.  Selected-entry
requests remain pending until all eligible linked-page transfers have
completed or fallen back to stored content.
@end table

  A successful request publishes a read-only buffer in Special mode,
including when the valid scope is empty.  Publication does not select a
window.  The ready hook can notify Lisp consumers after the result is
installed; its contract is described in @ref{Consuming Snapshots}.

  Starting another request cancels pending work from the same source.
Other source buffers are unaffected.  Killing the source or changing its
major mode also cancels pending work.  Completed snapshots have their
own lifetime; see @ref{Managing Snapshots}.

@node Managing Snapshots
@subsection Viewing and Discarding Snapshots
@cindex snapshot ownership
  Preparing a new snapshot does not kill earlier successful snapshots.
The source buffer remembers its latest result; each snapshot buffer
retains its own context.  Killing the source leaves completed snapshots
alive.

@deffn Command tessera-x-show-context
Display the latest successful snapshot associated with the current
buffer.  From a source buffer, select its latest snapshot; from a
snapshot buffer, select that snapshot.  Signal a user error if no live
snapshot buffer is associated with the current buffer.
@end deffn

@deffn Command tessera-x-discard-context
Kill the snapshot associated with the current source or snapshot buffer.
If it is the source's latest snapshot, clear the source's reference.
Signal a user error when there is no associated context.  Programs using
the snapshot buffer lose access to its contents.
@end deffn

  Discard an older snapshot from its own buffer without affecting the
source's latest snapshot.  Ordinary @code{kill-buffer} also removes a
snapshot; trying to show it afterward reports that no live result
exists.  Discarding a completed result does not cancel a preparation
already in progress.

@node Cancelling a Request
@subsection Cancellation
@cindex cancellation
  Cancellation stops pending preparation in one source buffer while
retaining its successful snapshots.  It prevents callbacks from
publishing the cancelled request as a new result.

@deffn Command tessera-x-cancel-context
Stop preparation in the current source buffer, including any pending
retrieval.  Retain successful snapshots.  Do nothing when that source
has no request in progress.
@end deffn

  Run this command in the source buffer.  It can stop an asynchronous
mu index query or linked-page download.  It cannot undo a completed Gnus
download or interrupt a synchronous operation currently occupying Emacs.
For automatic cancellation on a new request or source-buffer changes,
see @ref{Request States}.

@node Troubleshooting
@section Troubleshooting

  When a snapshot is unavailable or incomplete, first distinguish a
selection or request failure from an individual item's content note.
The following checks help locate the relevant setting or data source.

@table @asis
@item Preparation fails
Check that the native application is installed and configured and that
the command supports the current buffer.  Gnus needs an enabled Agent; a
download also needs an agentized method.  For a mu query failure, check
@code{mu4e-mu-binary}, @code{mu4e-mu-home}, and the reported error.  See
@ref{Requirements} and @ref{Retrieving Content}.

@item Expected records are missing
Check selection marks and the active region first.  For replies,
distinguish loaded results from local-index expansion; neither can find
records absent from its source.  Today scopes depend on the group,
query, or filter at point and the available local data.  Elfeed result
limits and date restrictions still apply.  See @ref{Selecting Content}.

@item Body text is absent or shorter than expected
Read content notes for missing files, absent Agent bodies, MIME errors,
and linked-page fallback.  Elfeed may skip a link because fetching is
disabled, the stored body reaches the threshold, or the URL is
ineligible.  Truncation notices instead indicate character budgets.
Metadata and notices can exceed the total budget.  See @ref{Retrieving
Content} and @ref{Snapshot Contents}.

@item Retrieval is slow
Elfeed's timeout applies to each active transfer, excluding queue time;
its concurrency limit applies to one request.  Gnus downloads are
synchronous.  Character budgets do not limit retrieval work.
See @ref{Retrieving Content} and @ref{Request States}.

@item No live snapshot can be shown
Check that preparation succeeded in this source buffer and that its
result buffer is still alive.  A failed or cancelled request does not
publish a replacement; another successful request changes the source's
latest reference.  See @ref{Managing Snapshots}.

@item A consumer does not receive the result
Check the ready hook's configuration, the request's completion state,
and errors from earlier hook functions.  See @ref{Consuming Snapshots}
for hook execution and failure handling.
@end table

@cindex bug reports
  Report reproducible problems at
@url{https://github.com/brsvh/emacs-tessera/issues}.  Include Emacs,
package and application versions, the command, relevant options, and
expected and observed behavior.  Include source material only when
needed and after removing private information.

@node Programming Contexts
@chapter Programming Contexts

  Lisp programs can consume a completed snapshot or produce snapshots
from another source.  This chapter describes those interfaces and their
ownership, completion, and cleanup contracts.  They belong to an
experimental package; private helpers containing @samp{--} are not
extension interfaces.

@menu
* Consuming Snapshots::  Reading completed contexts and using hooks.
* Producing Snapshots::  Supplying records and completing requests.
* Content Helpers: Content Helper Interfaces.  Text, message, and date helpers.
@end menu

@node Consuming Snapshots
@section Consuming Snapshots

  A @dfn{consumer} is Lisp code that reads or processes a completed
snapshot.  It can use the ready hook to run when preparation succeeds.

@defvar tessera-x-current-context
A buffer-local reference to the latest successful context from a source
buffer, initially @code{nil}.  In a snapshot buffer it refers to that
buffer's own context.  It does not refer to pending work.
@end defvar

  A non-@code{nil} reference alone does not prove that the snapshot
buffer still exists.  Check @code{buffer-live-p} on the result of
@code{tessera-x-context-buffer} before reading it.  Treat completed
context objects and their text as immutable.

  Publishing another snapshot does not kill the previous one.  Consumers
that need an older snapshot must retain their own references and arrange
to release it when no longer needed.  Ordinary @code{kill-buffer} can
leave a context object referring to a dead buffer.

@cindex ready hook
@defopt tessera-x-context-ready-hook
An abnormal hook, initially @code{nil}.  Each function receives one
argument, the completed @code{tessera-x-context}.  It runs once per
successful request in the originating source buffer, after the ready
object and snapshot buffer have been installed.
@end defopt

  The hook also runs for an empty successful snapshot.  Tessera does not
select a window when publishing, and it saves source point around the
hook.  Hook functions remain responsible for their own window changes or
other side effects.

  If a hook function signals an error, Tessera reports it without
invalidating the snapshot.  As with ordinary hook iteration, the error
can prevent later functions on that invocation from running.  Consumer
functions should handle their own expected failures.

  The following example saves a reference for a consumer in each source
buffer.  It does not send or display the text.  The @samp{my-} symbols
belong to user configuration, not to the Tessera API.

@lisp
(require 'tessera-x)

(defvar-local my-tessera-snapshot nil
  "Snapshot retained by my consumer.")

(defun my-tessera-remember (context)
  "Retain the completed CONTEXT for my consumer."
  (setq my-tessera-snapshot
        (tessera-x-context-buffer context)))

(add-hook 'tessera-x-context-ready-hook
          #'my-tessera-remember)
@end lisp

@noindent
After completion, use @code{buffer-live-p} before reading the retained
buffer with @code{with-current-buffer}.  Copy its text when a consumer
needs ownership independent of the buffer's lifetime.

@cindex gptel
  Applications such as gptel can consume snapshots through the ready
hook.  Install and configure the consumer separately.  Tessera X does
not depend on gptel or install consumer bindings; request submission and
response display belong to the consumer.

@node Producing Snapshots
@section Producing Snapshots

  A @dfn{producer} supplies records, starts a request in the source
buffer, obtains available content, and finishes the request.  For
asynchronous work it also registers cleanup and checks request identity
before handling a callback.

  Snapshot request objects are distinct from the entry contexts used to
render display rows.  @xref{Entry Contexts,,, tessera, Tessera}.

@menu
* Records: Source Items.  Source items and context objects.
* Request Lifecycle::   Starting, finishing, and cleaning up work.
* Producer Example::    A small synchronous snapshot producer.
@end menu

@node Source Items
@subsection Source Items and Context Objects

  Producers use one item record per source message or entry and a
context object for the request as a whole.  Fill records while the
request is pending; consumers read them after successful publication.

@cindex source items
@deftp {Data Type} tessera-x-item
A record supplied by a backend.  Construct one with
@code{make-tessera-x-item}, using keyword arguments for its fields.
Read fields with accessors named @samp{tessera-x-item-@var{field}}.  Use
@code{setf} to fill content while the request is pending.
@end deftp
@findex make-tessera-x-item
@findex tessera-x-item-p
@findex tessera-x-item-id
@findex tessera-x-item-message-id
@findex tessera-x-item-references
@findex tessera-x-item-parent
@findex tessera-x-item-subject
@findex tessera-x-item-date
@findex tessera-x-item-metadata
@findex tessera-x-item-group
@findex tessera-x-item-data
@findex tessera-x-item-body
@findex tessera-x-item-note
@table @code
@item id
A backend-local identity.  Use a stable value suitable for @code{equal}.

@item message-id
A normalized mail or news Message-ID, or @code{nil}.

@item references
A list of normalized reference identities in the source's order.

@item parent
An optional inferred native parent, kept separately from References.

@item subject
The full subject or title.

@item date
An Emacs time value, or @code{nil} when unknown.

@item metadata
An alist of printable field labels and values.

@item group
A provenance or group label for rendering.

@item data
Opaque backend retrieval data, such as a message path or entry URL.

@item body
Extracted text, or @code{nil} for no body.

@item note
An optional explanation of missing content or retrieval behavior.
@end table

  Construct separate records for a request.  The shared API does not
recursively copy items or protect their fields from later mutation.

@cindex context objects
@deftp {Data Type} tessera-x-context
A request and its eventual result.  Producers normally obtain it from
@code{tessera-x-context-start}; constructing the structure alone does
not install a live request in a source buffer.
@end deftp
@findex make-tessera-x-context
@findex tessera-x-context-p
@findex tessera-x-context-backend
@findex tessera-x-context-source
@findex tessera-x-context-scope
@findex tessera-x-context-items
@findex tessera-x-context-buffer
@findex tessera-x-context-created
@findex tessera-x-context-state
@findex tessera-x-context-cleanup
@findex tessera-x-context-error
@findex tessera-x-context-max-characters
@findex tessera-x-context-body-max-characters
  Accessors have the form @samp{tessera-x-context-@var{field}}.  The
fields @code{backend}, @code{source}, @code{scope}, @code{items}, and
@code{created} record the backend symbol, source buffer, scope label,
records, and start time.  @code{state} records the lifecycle state.
@code{buffer} becomes the published buffer on success; @code{error}
holds failure data when the request fails.

  The fields @code{max-characters} and @code{body-max-characters} hold
the budgets captured at start.  @code{cleanup} is a list of no-argument
resource cleanup functions.  Backend code may update pending items and
register cleanup; consumers should read ready results without changing
them.

@node Request Lifecycle
@subsection Request Lifecycle

  Start the request before retrieving content, then finish or fail it
after checking that it is still current.  Register resource cleanup
during preparation so every completion path can release it.

@defun tessera-x-context-start backend scope items
Start a request in the current buffer and return its context object.
@var{backend} is a symbol, @var{scope} describes the selection, and
@var{items} is a list of snapshotted @code{tessera-x-item} records.
Cancel this source's previous pending request, retain its latest ready
snapshot, capture the budgets and time, and arrange cancellation when
the source is killed or changes major mode.
@end defun

@defun tessera-x-context-pending-p context
Return non-@code{nil} only if @var{context} has state @code{pending},
its source buffer is live, and it is still that source's pending
request.
@end defun

  A state test alone is insufficient for a callback.  Use the predicate
to reject obsolete work, and release any response resources even when
the work is no longer current.

@defun tessera-x-context-finish context
Publish @var{context} if it is still pending and current, and return
@var{context}.  Render its text into a new read-only buffer, set its
state to @code{ready}, release registered resources, update the source
reference, and invoke the ready hook.  A stale or finished context is
returned without being published again.
@end defun

  Rendering errors kill the incomplete result buffer, fail the request,
and are signalled to the caller.  A producer should handle errors from
its own retrieval and completion path.  Completion itself does not
select a window or move the source's point or region.

@cindex resource cleanup
@defun tessera-x-context-fail context error-data
If @var{context} is still current and pending, record @var{error-data},
set state to @code{failed}, clear the source's pending request, release
registered resources, and report the failure in the echo area.  Retain
the previous successful snapshot.  Ignore obsolete requests.
@end defun

  Register cleanup functions in @code{tessera-x-context-cleanup} as soon
as resources exist.  They take no arguments and must not assume that the
current buffer is the source.  Cleanup runs on success, failure, and
cancellation.  Its list is cleared before invocation, and an error in
one cleanup function is reported while remaining cleanup functions are
still attempted.

  A backend should distinguish an unreadable individual body from an
unusable request.  Retain the item's metadata and a note for the former;
fail the request when its scope cannot be queried or its result cannot
be published.

@node Producer Example
@subsection A Producer Example

  This small synchronous producer demonstrates ownership and completion.
It is suitable for evaluating in a scratch buffer after loading
@code{tessera-x}.  It creates a snapshot for that buffer and invokes any
configured ready hooks.

@lisp
(require 'tessera-x)
(let* ((item (make-tessera-x-item
              :id "example-1"
              :subject "Example source"
              :date (current-time)
              :body "An example body."))
       (context (tessera-x-context-start
                 'example "One example record" (list item))))
  (condition-case err
      (tessera-x-context-finish context)
    (error
     (tessera-x-context-fail context
                             (error-message-string err)))))
@end lisp

@node Content Helper Interfaces
@section Content Helper Interfaces

  Snapshot producers can use these functions to extract text, follow
message relationships, and select records by date.  They operate on
supplied text and records rather than display rows.

@menu
* Text Conversion: Reading Raw Messages.  Extracting message and HTML text.
* Message Relations: Message Relationships.  Identities, descendants, and conversations.
* Local Day Boundaries::  Finding the current calendar day.
@end menu

@node Reading Raw Messages
@subsection Reading and Converting Content

  These helpers extract headers and body text from supplied messages or
HTML.  Retrieval remains the producer's responsibility.

@defun tessera-x-read-message item reader
Call the no-argument function @var{reader} in a temporary unibyte
buffer.  It must insert a complete raw message, including headers.
Decode available From, To, Cc, Keywords, and X-GM-LABELS fields into
@var{item}'s metadata and extract its MIME body and attachment
information.  Record failures as content notes and return @var{item}.
@end defun

@defun tessera-x-html-text html
Return a plain-text rendering of the string @var{html}, using the
conversion behavior described in @ref{Text Extraction}.
@end defun

  Neither function selects a source window.  The reader is responsible
for obtaining its input and must signal an error when it cannot insert a
usable message.

@node Message Relationships
@subsection Message Relationships

  Normalize identities and retain native parent relationships before
selecting descendants or grouping conversations.  These operations use
the supplied records; they do not query a mail server or index.

@defun tessera-x-message-ids value
Normalize message identifiers from a string, list, or vector.  Split
string members at whitespace, remove surrounding angle brackets from
each token, ignore non-string members, and return a list of strings.
This is normalization, not a complete message-header parser.
@end defun

@defun tessera-x-native-parent-stack identity level stack
Return an updated native ancestry stack for @var{identity} at
@var{level}.  @var{stack} contains @code{(LEVEL . IDENTITY)} pairs,
deepest first.  Remove preceding entries at the same or deeper level and
push the new pair; the second pair then identifies its parent.
@end defun

  Visit every native record, including folded and unselected records,
when using the stack.  Selection must not accidentally reparent a
record.  Retain native inferred ancestry in @code{parent} without
rewriting the original References list.

@defun tessera-x-subthread items anchor
Return @var{anchor} and its descendants among @var{items}, in their
input order.  The anchor should be an item in that list.  Follow
References and native parents using Message-ID, falling back to the
backend identity when it is absent.  Missing intermediate parents do not
prevent a reference to an earlier ancestor from connecting a record.
Cycles do not cause an endless traversal.
@end defun

  This function does not retrieve additional records or infer ancestry
from equal subjects.  The backend determines whether @var{items} comes
from loaded results or a wider local index.

@defun tessera-x-group-threads items
Return @var{items} grouped by connected References and native parent
identities.  Preserve first-group and within-group order.  Update each
item's @code{group} field to the first member's subject.  Do not merge
records solely because their subjects match.
@end defun

  This operation mutates item group fields and belongs before
publication.  It preserves separate metadata fields such as the original
newsgroup or maildir.

@node Local Day Boundaries
@subsection Local Day Boundaries
@cindex local day boundaries
  Use calendar boundaries to select records from today's local date,
including days whose length changes with daylight saving time.

@defun tessera-x-today-bounds
Return a cons of local midnight and the following local midnight as
Emacs time values.  Compute calendar boundaries in the current local
time zone, rather than adding a fixed number of seconds.
@end defun

  Gnus and Elfeed use the half-open interval from the first boundary up
to, but excluding, the second.  mu4e instead delegates date selection to
its native @samp{date:today..now} query.

